# Java Security Requirements

## Purpose
Define baseline secure-coding requirements for Java projects, including those that execute external tools.

## Rules

### JSEC-001 · Validate input at trust boundaries (MUST)
Validate untrusted input against expected type, length, range, format and permitted values. Parsing alone is not validation.
**Verification:** tests covering invalid, hostile and boundary inputs.

### JSEC-002 · No embedded secrets (MUST)
Do not commit credentials, access tokens, private keys or passwords in code, configuration examples or test fixtures. Supply secrets via an approved external mechanism.
**Verification:** secret scanning in CI.

### JSEC-003 · Safe command execution (MUST)
When starting external programs, pass executable and arguments as discrete values (for example, via `ProcessBuilder`) instead of concatenating untrusted input into shell command strings. Apply allowlists to executable paths when appropriate.
**Verification:** code review and security-focused tests.

### JSEC-004 · Filesystem safety (MUST)
Normalize and validate paths relative to an authorized working root when processing external paths. Prevent traversal, unsafe overwrites and unintended access through symbolic links as applicable to the threat model.
**Verification:** traversal and symlink tests.

### JSEC-005 · Dependencies (MUST)
Track third-party components and their versions; evaluate known vulnerabilities before release. Document accepted risks and their treatment.
**Verification:** dependency inventory and vulnerability scan.

### JSEC-006 · Least privilege (MUST)
Do not require administrator/root privileges for operations that can run with lesser permissions; isolate elevated actions and document the justification.
**Verification:** permission review and negative tests.

### JSEC-007 · Sensitive data and logs (MUST)
Never disclose secrets in errors, logs or diagnostic exports. Apply data minimization to personal and confidential information.
**Verification:** tests and review.

### JSEC-008 · Safe defaults (SHOULD)
Prefer deny-by-default permissions, explicit opt-in for destructive operations and clear confirmation for irreversible changes.
**Verification:** acceptance tests.

## References
- Oracle Secure Coding Guidelines for Java SE
- OWASP Cheat Sheet Series (Java Security, Input Validation, OS Command Injection)

References are informative; rule IDs above define the obligations in this document.
